WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:05.970
&gt;&gt; Hi, good afternoon.

00:00:05.971 --> 00:00:08.100
We are from Bempu Health.

00:00:08.101 --> 00:00:11.285
We are a social enterprise
based out of Bangalore.

00:00:11.286 --> 00:00:13.515
We make health interventions in

00:00:13.516 --> 00:00:16.565
the neonatal and maternal
health care space.

00:00:16.566 --> 00:00:18.515
This is our first product.

00:00:18.516 --> 00:00:22.210
This is a hypothermia bracelet
for newborn babies,

00:00:22.211 --> 00:00:26.280
which we have
managed to deploy to

00:00:26.281 --> 00:00:28.850
various states in India and some

00:00:28.851 --> 00:00:32.235
countries with the help
of Gates Foundation,

00:00:32.236 --> 00:00:35.010
USAID, and Saving Lives of Birth.

00:00:35.011 --> 00:00:37.675
With UNICEF. We reached Zimbabwe,

00:00:37.676 --> 00:00:40.690
Benin, and Papua New Guinea.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.890
So, while we were
working on our bracelet,

00:00:43.891 --> 00:00:46.020
we also identified
various other needs.

00:00:46.021 --> 00:00:48.040
So we have a portfolio
of products.

00:00:48.041 --> 00:00:50.460
Today, we'll be focusing
on the Care Cradle.

00:00:50.461 --> 00:00:54.105
So, the Care Cradle is
a hybrid product service,

00:00:54.106 --> 00:00:59.985
which monitors babies,

00:00:59.986 --> 00:01:02.120
high-risk babies
at home after they

00:01:02.121 --> 00:01:05.135
get discharged from
the neonatal ICU.

00:01:05.136 --> 00:01:07.950
This is usually in
low resource settings.

00:01:07.951 --> 00:01:09.875
So, just to give
you some context,

00:01:09.876 --> 00:01:14.925
there are 750,000 newborns
who die each year in India.

00:01:14.926 --> 00:01:19.520
Critical neonates, especially
in low resource settings,

00:01:19.521 --> 00:01:22.100
are discharged early from
their facilities because

00:01:22.101 --> 00:01:25.145
of the lack of beds,
available beds.

00:01:25.146 --> 00:01:28.435
The high-risk period
is thus spent

00:01:28.436 --> 00:01:32.800
in uneducated and
geographically isolated homes.

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:37.965
So, again, because the
parents are not well-aware,

00:01:37.966 --> 00:01:39.680
they are not usually taken to

00:01:39.681 --> 00:01:43.040
the facility if the parents
are unable to identify

00:01:43.041 --> 00:01:44.945
symptoms that could indicate

00:01:44.946 --> 00:01:48.100
eventual sepsis mortality
and other things.

00:01:48.101 --> 00:01:50.630
So, they develop illness at home,

00:01:50.631 --> 00:01:54.120
and this could lead to, yes.

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:59.020
So, what we did was
through this cradle,

00:01:59.021 --> 00:02:00.950
we collect clinical data.

00:02:00.951 --> 00:02:02.220
This is through a smartphone,

00:02:02.221 --> 00:02:03.815
which is on top of the cradle.

00:02:03.816 --> 00:02:07.200
We collect videos
four times a day.

00:02:07.201 --> 00:02:08.815
We ask the parents to record

00:02:08.816 --> 00:02:10.370
a two and half minute video with

00:02:10.371 --> 00:02:12.845
the press of a button,
four times a day.

00:02:12.846 --> 00:02:14.760
We also have
a vital sign monitor,

00:02:14.761 --> 00:02:16.280
which is placed on
the baby, which collects

00:02:16.281 --> 00:02:18.320
the heart rate and the SPO2.

00:02:18.321 --> 00:02:20.900
Nurses call the parents for

00:02:20.901 --> 00:02:24.970
an audio account for
information about feeding,

00:02:24.971 --> 00:02:28.025
stool urine outputs, etc.

00:02:28.026 --> 00:02:30.020
All of that information
is collected on

00:02:30.021 --> 00:02:31.070
our dashboard to make a

00:02:31.071 --> 00:02:33.140
comprehensive assessment
about the baby's health.

00:02:33.141 --> 00:02:36.105
So, now we have a team of
clinicians on the back-end who

00:02:36.106 --> 00:02:39.510
review these symptoms
for the duration signs.

00:02:39.511 --> 00:02:41.795
Then they alert the family,

00:02:41.796 --> 00:02:44.785
or the health worker in
the area is alerted.

00:02:44.786 --> 00:02:46.820
So, this is how the system works.

00:02:46.821 --> 00:02:49.020
I will talk to you
about the data.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:53.150
&gt;&gt; So, using a prototype,

00:02:53.151 --> 00:02:54.590
which looks like this,

00:02:54.591 --> 00:02:56.930
we've tried this program out.

00:02:56.931 --> 00:03:01.340
We've piloted it in Karnataka
in Shimoga District,

00:03:01.341 --> 00:03:04.170
in Telangana, and in
Bangalore as well.

00:03:04.171 --> 00:03:06.280
So, we have used cases from

00:03:06.281 --> 00:03:07.920
rural and urban India and

00:03:07.921 --> 00:03:09.860
we've seen a lot
of positive value.

00:03:09.861 --> 00:03:13.530
So, we've tried this
with about 100 babies,

00:03:13.531 --> 00:03:15.145
and in those 100 babies,

00:03:15.146 --> 00:03:19.270
we've detected five cases
of readmission.

00:03:19.271 --> 00:03:22.620
So, this includes readmission
to tertiary care,

00:03:22.621 --> 00:03:24.715
due to illnesses like jaundice,

00:03:24.716 --> 00:03:28.325
sepsis, respiratory
distress, etc.

00:03:28.326 --> 00:03:32.165
Even illnesses that don't
require readmission,

00:03:32.166 --> 00:03:34.640
but just a revisit
to the facility,

00:03:34.641 --> 00:03:36.680
can be diagnosed
through signs like

00:03:36.681 --> 00:03:40.105
skin color or dropping
the feeding pattern of the baby.

00:03:40.106 --> 00:03:41.760
Any sign of dehydration,

00:03:41.761 --> 00:03:44.110
any sign of high
respiratory rate,

00:03:44.111 --> 00:03:45.850
or chest indrawing, etc.

00:03:45.851 --> 00:03:48.710
So, we see that there
is positive value,

00:03:48.711 --> 00:03:50.090
but it's difficult to scale

00:03:50.091 --> 00:03:52.519
this program because
of certain challenges.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:54.320
So, one major challenge is

00:03:54.321 --> 00:03:56.170
that we require
skilled human resource.

00:03:56.171 --> 00:03:57.710
Currently, the clinical team

00:03:57.711 --> 00:04:00.315
consists of nurses
and neonatologists,

00:04:00.316 --> 00:04:03.890
with significant experience
in neonatal care.

00:04:03.891 --> 00:04:07.100
So, the first round of
assessment is done by nurses,

00:04:07.101 --> 00:04:08.810
and if there's something
that is deemed to

00:04:08.811 --> 00:04:10.670
be wrong with
the health of the baby,

00:04:10.671 --> 00:04:13.080
this is escalated to
a neonatologists.

00:04:13.081 --> 00:04:15.290
So, each packet of
data would need

00:04:15.291 --> 00:04:19.000
at least 15 minutes from
a nurse to be reviewed.

00:04:19.001 --> 00:04:22.385
So, effectively, one nurse
on a working shift

00:04:22.386 --> 00:04:26.810
could only see about five
to seven babies per day.

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:30.590
Yes. So, there's the time
taken to monitor this data,

00:04:30.591 --> 00:04:32.060
also time taken to collect

00:04:32.061 --> 00:04:33.880
the data in the form
of audio accounts,

00:04:33.881 --> 00:04:35.410
which is speaking to parents,

00:04:35.411 --> 00:04:37.790
asking simple
follow-up questions.

00:04:37.791 --> 00:04:39.950
There's also a lot
of subjectivity.

00:04:39.951 --> 00:04:42.260
So, as you might have seen
in the previous slides,

00:04:42.261 --> 00:04:43.760
there's an example of the images

00:04:43.761 --> 00:04:45.720
that we receive
through these videos.

00:04:45.721 --> 00:04:47.295
So, through these videos,
the nurses are

00:04:47.296 --> 00:04:49.780
expected to assess the skin
color of the babies,

00:04:49.781 --> 00:04:51.800
the chest movement, and this

00:04:51.801 --> 00:04:55.635
would depend on the skill level
of the nurses itself.

00:04:55.636 --> 00:04:59.440
Also on ambient lighting the way
the video was shot, etc.

00:04:59.441 --> 00:05:02.600
So, factors that could
be controlled and

00:05:02.601 --> 00:05:04.700
require of reduction in

00:05:04.701 --> 00:05:07.739
subjectivity when it
comes to analysis.

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:10.620
So, this is our vision

00:05:10.621 --> 00:05:13.010
of how we think
technology could help

00:05:13.011 --> 00:05:15.140
us in scaling
this intervention and

00:05:15.141 --> 00:05:17.515
reaching across to more babies.

00:05:17.516 --> 00:05:19.860
One would be risk assessment.

00:05:19.861 --> 00:05:21.830
So, we think that we could make

00:05:21.831 --> 00:05:23.780
better use of
the skilled workforce

00:05:23.781 --> 00:05:27.680
simply by doing a
preliminary assessments.

00:05:27.681 --> 00:05:31.185
So, such that the cases that
require origin detention,

00:05:31.186 --> 00:05:33.690
reach the assessor first.

00:05:33.691 --> 00:05:38.050
Also, flagging
some severe danger signs,

00:05:38.051 --> 00:05:40.605
which do not even require
a clinical assessment,

00:05:40.606 --> 00:05:42.620
but would simply
require medical care.

00:05:42.621 --> 00:05:46.025
So, this would reduce
response times in such a system.

00:05:46.026 --> 00:05:53.120
Then we can also improve
on the workflow.

00:05:53.121 --> 00:05:55.015
So, if our nurse sees

00:05:55.016 --> 00:05:57.325
that a baby is falling
ill, typically,

00:05:57.326 --> 00:05:59.070
we ask follow-up questions like,

00:05:59.071 --> 00:06:02.065
"What was the feeding pattern
in the last 24 hours?"

00:06:02.066 --> 00:06:04.760
So, we try to collect
information about

00:06:04.761 --> 00:06:06.510
the current medical behavior and

00:06:06.511 --> 00:06:08.555
also past history of the baby,

00:06:08.556 --> 00:06:10.549
which we think could be automated

00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:12.975
again to increase response times.

00:06:12.976 --> 00:06:16.520
Also, once we prescribe
a line of treatment,

00:06:16.521 --> 00:06:18.275
there could be more
effective ways

00:06:18.276 --> 00:06:20.240
of sending reminders and

00:06:20.241 --> 00:06:21.800
tracking if
the treatment is being

00:06:21.801 --> 00:06:25.350
followed through
technological systems.

00:06:25.351 --> 00:06:27.890
In terms of the video
assessment, simply,

00:06:27.891 --> 00:06:31.160
also we think that there
could be data processing such

00:06:31.161 --> 00:06:33.290
that preliminary assessments can

00:06:33.291 --> 00:06:35.790
be done for respiratory rate,

00:06:35.791 --> 00:06:38.655
image analysis, can be
done to see skin color,

00:06:38.656 --> 00:06:41.390
reduced subjectivity, and shorten

00:06:41.391 --> 00:06:45.140
the time taken to review
these 2.5 minute long videos.

00:06:45.141 --> 00:06:51.050
Yes. So, the data that
we have right now,

00:06:51.051 --> 00:06:54.500
so we start from the day of
enrollment in the study rate.

00:06:54.501 --> 00:06:56.650
So, we have a lot
of baseline data.

00:06:56.651 --> 00:06:58.770
So, we know factors about

00:06:58.771 --> 00:07:01.520
the baby's birth like
the weight at birth,

00:07:01.521 --> 00:07:03.530
the gestational age,
how long the baby

00:07:03.531 --> 00:07:06.110
has spent time in
a tertiary care facility?

00:07:06.111 --> 00:07:07.805
What kind of care did it receive

00:07:07.806 --> 00:07:09.440
at the tertiary facility?

00:07:09.441 --> 00:07:11.720
Then, we also have
demographic information

00:07:11.721 --> 00:07:13.575
about what was the age
of this mother?

00:07:13.576 --> 00:07:15.850
Is this the mother's
first pregnancy?

00:07:15.851 --> 00:07:18.370
What kind of a household
does the baby come from?

00:07:18.371 --> 00:07:21.820
Then, in terms of the daily
monitoring, we have videos.

00:07:21.821 --> 00:07:24.500
So, we have
detailed analysis from

00:07:24.501 --> 00:07:26.120
these care nurses about what

00:07:26.121 --> 00:07:28.310
was the activity level
in the video?

00:07:28.311 --> 00:07:29.690
Was the baby crying?

00:07:29.691 --> 00:07:31.845
Was the baby crying loud enough?

00:07:31.846 --> 00:07:34.115
Was there any chest indrawing?

00:07:34.116 --> 00:07:36.220
Then, through
our vital signs monitor,

00:07:36.221 --> 00:07:38.510
we have data about
the average heart rate,

00:07:38.511 --> 00:07:40.485
in the 2.5 minute video,

00:07:40.486 --> 00:07:43.140
and also the oxygen saturation.

00:07:43.141 --> 00:07:45.070
Then, through the audio accounts,

00:07:45.071 --> 00:07:46.455
we have details about,

00:07:46.456 --> 00:07:48.220
in the last six to eight hours,

00:07:48.221 --> 00:07:50.340
how often has the baby been fed?

00:07:50.341 --> 00:07:52.930
How often has the baby
passed urine or stool?

00:07:52.931 --> 00:07:55.590
Does the mother see
any abnormalities?

00:07:55.591 --> 00:07:57.905
Has she seen any
convulsive movements?

00:07:57.906 --> 00:07:59.630
Has she seen anything
on the skin of

00:07:59.631 --> 00:08:02.880
the baby that could
potentially be problematic?

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:08.150
Yes. So, that's the end
of our presentation.

00:08:08.151 --> 00:08:11.275
We do have some ideas on how
we could leverage the data.

00:08:11.276 --> 00:08:14.830
We're excited to discuss it
in the following sessions.

00:08:14.831 --> 00:08:16.580
&gt;&gt; Anyone with questions?

00:08:16.581 --> 00:08:19.695
&gt;&gt; You said you were
compiling this,

00:08:19.696 --> 00:08:24.825
how many babies has
these technology been used with?

00:08:24.826 --> 00:08:28.280
&gt;&gt; We've used it with
a little over 100 babies

00:08:28.281 --> 00:08:33.130
in Karnataka State and
Telangana State of India.

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:46.120
Yeah.

00:08:46.121 --> 00:08:47.950
&gt;&gt; Hi. What device

00:08:47.951 --> 00:08:49.840
are you using currently
for video monitoring?

00:08:49.841 --> 00:08:51.355
Are you using a mobile phone?

00:08:51.356 --> 00:08:54.550
&gt;&gt; Yes. For our pilot projects,

00:08:54.551 --> 00:08:56.990
we're just using a smartphone
because that enables

00:08:56.991 --> 00:08:59.450
us to control the quality
of video that comes in,

00:08:59.451 --> 00:09:01.040
and also this packet of data can

00:09:01.041 --> 00:09:02.830
be sent through
cellular networks.

00:09:02.831 --> 00:09:04.390
So, yeah, we are using
a smartphone right now.

00:09:04.391 --> 00:09:05.810
&gt;&gt; But won't mobile phones be

00:09:05.811 --> 00:09:07.300
contaminated most of the time?

00:09:07.301 --> 00:09:11.790
Because there was
a study a few days back,

00:09:11.791 --> 00:09:15.380
talking about how 101 out of
105 mobile phones that they

00:09:15.381 --> 00:09:19.285
studied was contaminated with
bacteria in health care.

00:09:19.286 --> 00:09:22.950
&gt;&gt; Okay. Can you go back to
the slide with the picture?

00:09:23.770 --> 00:09:27.360
So, this is what the product
looks like right now.

00:09:27.361 --> 00:09:30.160
So, the smartphone is placed
on top of this stand,

00:09:30.161 --> 00:09:34.005
which is at least
40-centimeter above the baby.

00:09:34.006 --> 00:09:36.040
So, there's no point of time when

00:09:36.041 --> 00:09:38.635
the baby is in contact
with the phone.

00:09:38.636 --> 00:09:41.475
I hope that answers
your question.

00:09:41.476 --> 00:09:43.840
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Okay.

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:47.260
&gt;&gt; We take one more question,

00:09:47.261 --> 00:09:48.760
and then we'll have a break one

00:09:48.761 --> 00:09:51.050
session to discuss this further.

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:54.020
&gt;&gt; I guess you guys are
doing what sounds like

00:09:54.021 --> 00:09:56.240
a relatively small
scale pilot right now,

00:09:56.241 --> 00:09:58.190
but just in terms of thinking

00:09:58.191 --> 00:10:00.635
about how you might want
this to be implemented,

00:10:00.636 --> 00:10:03.155
who would you imagine actually

00:10:03.156 --> 00:10:05.640
buying and having this resource,

00:10:05.641 --> 00:10:08.220
and how do you
imagine it be used?

00:10:08.221 --> 00:10:12.310
&gt;&gt; Okay. So, you

00:10:12.311 --> 00:10:14.030
saw the products that we've

00:10:14.031 --> 00:10:16.830
developed before which is
the hypothermia bracelet.

00:10:16.831 --> 00:10:18.365
So, for that product,

00:10:18.366 --> 00:10:20.300
we have used government channels

00:10:20.301 --> 00:10:21.560
as well as private channels.

00:10:21.561 --> 00:10:22.640
So, we see a system,

00:10:22.641 --> 00:10:24.690
where we would use this,

00:10:24.691 --> 00:10:27.140
we would integrate
our clinical team with

00:10:27.141 --> 00:10:30.490
the tertiary level facilities
in district hospitals.

00:10:30.491 --> 00:10:32.390
We would be enrolling
babies that are

00:10:32.391 --> 00:10:34.060
discharged from these facilities.

00:10:34.061 --> 00:10:38.135
So, we expect that the
government would work with us to

00:10:38.136 --> 00:10:39.795
subsidize the care that

00:10:39.796 --> 00:10:41.360
this system could offer to

00:10:41.361 --> 00:10:43.865
these babies being discharged
from the hospital.

00:10:43.866 --> 00:10:46.200
Also in the private cases,

00:10:46.201 --> 00:10:49.235
even in Tier-2 and
Tier-3 cities of India.

00:10:49.236 --> 00:10:50.810
So, remote assessment would

00:10:50.811 --> 00:10:52.940
significantly reduce the cost,

00:10:52.941 --> 00:10:56.380
and increase the quality of
care that is accessible.

00:10:56.630 --> 00:11:01.395
&gt;&gt; So, Villant you had
one question. We'll allow.

00:11:01.396 --> 00:11:02.370
&gt;&gt; I had one more
question please.

00:11:02.371 --> 00:11:03.840
&gt;&gt; Okay, we can take two.

00:11:03.841 --> 00:11:05.250
&gt;&gt; I think in
the interest of moving

00:11:05.251 --> 00:11:06.705
to the break up, I'll hold it.

00:11:06.706 --> 00:11:08.475
&gt;&gt; Fantastic. We'll come to you.

00:11:08.476 --> 00:11:10.950
&gt;&gt; So, you mentioned

00:11:10.951 --> 00:11:13.380
that there are audio
accounts of these.

00:11:13.381 --> 00:11:19.905
So, are these in local languages
or they have mixed,

00:11:19.906 --> 00:11:24.040
code mixed, or what kind
of accounts are they?

00:11:24.440 --> 00:11:27.650
&gt;&gt; They are largely in
local languages because

00:11:27.651 --> 00:11:29.240
60 percent of our used cases

00:11:29.241 --> 00:11:30.515
are currently in rural India.

00:11:30.516 --> 00:11:32.180
So, they would be
in local languages.

00:11:32.181 --> 00:11:35.410
&gt;&gt; [inaudible].

00:11:35.411 --> 00:11:35.740
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:11:35.741 --> 00:11:39.125
&gt;&gt; So, currently, you would
get them transcribed by?

00:11:39.126 --> 00:11:40.970
&gt;&gt; Not transcribed, but they

00:11:40.971 --> 00:11:42.340
would be assessed on the call.

00:11:42.341 --> 00:11:44.299
So, we don't note down everything

00:11:44.300 --> 00:11:46.640
that is relayed by the parents,

00:11:46.641 --> 00:11:49.290
but we would only note down
the frequency of feeds.

00:11:49.291 --> 00:11:50.480
We'd convert that into

00:11:50.481 --> 00:11:52.770
as many quantitative
values as possible.

00:11:52.771 --> 00:11:55.665
&gt;&gt; Okay. Is that
interactive? The-

00:11:55.666 --> 00:11:57.450
&gt;&gt; Yes. It's a phone call.

00:11:57.451 --> 00:11:59.380
&gt;&gt; Okay. Thank you.

